What is Inheritance(상속)?
	Inheritance is an object-oriented programming concept where a new class can reuse the attributes and methods of an existing class.
	A class that inherits from another class is called a child class or subclass, and the original class is called the parent class or base class.
	This allows developers to build new classes on top of existing functionality instead of writing everything from scratch.

	--EXAMPLE--
	class SVC:
		def research(self):
			print("We research")
	
	class CIAL(SVC):
		pass
	-----------
	In the example above, CIAL inherits from SVC.
	Even though CIAL does not define its own research() method, it can still use it because that method is inherited from the parent class.

	"Why inheritance is useful"
	- promotes code reuse
	- reduces duplication
	- makes code easier to organize
	- allows cutomization by extending existing classes.
	* A child class can keep the features of the parent class and also add its own new behavior.

----------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------
nn.Module in PyTorch
	In PyTorch, nn.Module is the base class for neural network models.
	When defining a custom model, it is comon to inherit from nn.Module.
	This mean becomes a PyTorch model class and can use the built-in features provided by nn.Module.

	"Why inherit from nn.Module?"
	* to gains important PyTorch functionality such as ~
	- automatic registration of alyers and parameters
	- support for model.parameters()
	- support for model.state_dict()
	- support for model.train() and model.eval()
	- support for moving the model to CPU or GPU with model.to(device)

	"Why super().__init__() is needed?"
	* Inside the constructor, super().__init() calls the constructor of the parent class.
	* This step initializes the internal sturcture of nn.Module so that PyTorch can properly manage layers, parameters, and other model-related features.
